    "https://www.paulsmiths.edu/careers/?jobId=f156349a-759d-1d94-6c75-7cc141257074": {
      "title": "Assistant Men's Basketball Coach",
      "description": "Careers - Paul Smith's College Skip Main Navigation Paul Smith's College Home For You For You Audience Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center & Institutes Centers Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Menu You are here: Home / Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College At Paul Smith’s College, we believe in investing in our employees’ well-being. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that includes not only robust health, dental, and vision insurance, but generous paid time off, including vacation, personal leave, sick leave, and holidays. We prioritize employee wellness through access to a supportive employee assistance program. A myriad of voluntary benefits are available, including short- & long-term disability insurance, accident insurance, critical illness insurance, and legal assistance. We understand the importance of work-life balance and encourage employees to take advantage of our flexible work options when possible. The College offers eligible employees with a 403(b) retirement plan account and contributes 5% of their salary towards the plan. Employees receive complimentary access to our library, gym, athletic facilities, fitness center, recreational equipment, on-campus sporting events, cultural events, and social activities. Discounts are available for on-campus dining and the college’s Mercantile bookstore. We value our employees’ academic and personal interests and offer tuition remission for employees and their dependents. Employees receive complimentary access to the Paul Smith’s College Visitor Interpretive Center (the VIC), which offers 25 miles of scenic hiking, snowshoeing, and cross-country ski trails, wildlife viewing opportunities, educational programs, and interactive butterfly exhibit. For more information or questions about careers at Paul Smith’s College, please call us at: 518-327-6179 Back to Job Openings Assistant Men's Basketball Coach Apply Share Link copied Position: Assistant Men’s Basketball Coach & Head Coach for JV Men’s Basketball Status: Part Time/Seasonal Department: Athletics Report to: Head Men’s Basketball Coach PT Salary: $12,000/year The College: Paul Smith’s College is a small, private baccalaureate and associate degree granting institution located on a 170-acre campus adjacent to Lower St. Regis Lake in the 6 million acre Adirondack Park of northern New York State. The College is committed to providing all students with an outstanding undergraduate experience through engaged teaching, experiential learning, and personal attention. Paul Smith’s College seeks student athletes who will represent the college and their sport well, and who are as motivated to succeed in the classroom as they are in competition. Position Description: Reporting to the Head Men’s Basketball Coach, the Assistant Men’s Basketball Coach & Head Coach for JV Men’s Basketball provides leadership in developing an effective basketball program. The assistant coach will work with the Head Coach to help facilitate effective practice sessions for the varsity program while developing workouts, practice sessions, team bonding activities, logistics, schedules, and other necessities for the junior varsity program. In addition, the position assists with recruiting student-athletes for the two teams and provides skills development opportunities for the program’s players. Close cooperation with the Office of Admissions is expected as is adherence to policies and procedures set forth by the USCAA, NCAA regulations, and the YSCC. Additional Duties & Responsibilities: Represents the department at campus and community events. Assists with the Department’s strategic initiatives and special projects. Helps promote College athletics and facilities throughout the community. Coordinates setup and take down at home athletic contests (game day management) as needed. Works effectively with officials, the athletic trainer, and visiting team coaches. Trains and schedules student workers as needed. Assures a safe and enjoyable experience for visiting teams and spectators. Serves as a role model for student athletes. Requirements: Bachelor’s Degree required; Masters in Sports Management or related field preferred. One year as a high school, AAU or college basketball coach; comparable experience may be considered. Proven ability to recruit student athletes. Experience in the field of media relations or public information using traditional as well as digital media. Knowledgeable in compiling statistics and statistical reports; professional experience specific to sports information preferred. Strong communication, interpersonal and technology skills. Effective both as a leader and as a collaborator. Record of teaching and modeling character, values, and responsibility. Ability to work with a broad range of diverse individuals and groups to form a safe and inclusive campus community. Flexible schedule to include evenings, weekends and travel as required. Possess or is willing to complete CPR and First Aid certification; Driver’s license and driving record acceptable to our insurance carrier. Employment may be subject to a background check. Paul Smith’s College is an equal opportunity employer. Position descriptions are created that focus on essential functions, using inclusive language, and avoid unnecessary or discriminatory requirements. To Apply: Applicants must submit a cover letter, resume and contact information for three professional references. Electronic submissions are strongly encouraged and should be sent in Microsoft Word or Pdf format to: employment@paulsmiths.edu Mailing address: People & Culture Recruitment Manager, Paul Smith’s College, PO Box 265, Paul Smiths, NY 12970. Faxed to: (518) 327-6161. Apply Paul Smith's College Home 7777 NY-30, Paul Smiths, NY 12970 admissions@paulsmiths.edu Phone 888-873-6570 60° F Clear sky Social Facebook Instagram X YouTube TikTok Live Webcams Action Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login Footer About Paul Smith's College Faculty & Staff Directory Graduate Studies Careers Mercantile Library Directions to Paul Smith's College Privacy Policy Request Info News © 2026 Paul Smith's College . All rights reserved Site Menu Search Submit site The Smitty Story Apply for Admission First-Year Applicants Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants International Applicants Graduate Applicants Cost & Aid Tuition & Fees Net Price Calculator Types of Aid Applying for Aid Payment Academics Find a Program Hands-on Learning Work Experience Program Academic Support Meet Our Faculty Graduate Studies M.S. Natural Resources Conservation M.P.S. Sustainable Tourism Management Graduate Certificates Battlefish Academy TopGuide Troops to Timber Life at Paul Smith’s College Housing & Dining Campus Community The Adirondacks Safety & Support Alumni Athletics Search Submit Actions Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login For You Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Close Close"
    },
    "https://www.paulsmiths.edu/careers/?jobId=c101cb89-3e38-85d5-34da-560183ab4c36": {
      "title": "Seasonal Lean-tos, Cabins & Trails Crew Member",
      "description": "Careers - Paul Smith's College Skip Main Navigation Paul Smith's College Home For You For You Audience Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center & Institutes Centers Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Menu You are here: Home / Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College At Paul Smith’s College, we believe in investing in our employees’ well-being. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that includes not only robust health, dental, and vision insurance, but generous paid time off, including vacation, personal leave, sick leave, and holidays. We prioritize employee wellness through access to a supportive employee assistance program. A myriad of voluntary benefits are available, including short- & long-term disability insurance, accident insurance, critical illness insurance, and legal assistance. We understand the importance of work-life balance and encourage employees to take advantage of our flexible work options when possible. The College offers eligible employees with a 403(b) retirement plan account and contributes 5% of their salary towards the plan. Employees receive complimentary access to our library, gym, athletic facilities, fitness center, recreational equipment, on-campus sporting events, cultural events, and social activities. Discounts are available for on-campus dining and the college’s Mercantile bookstore. We value our employees’ academic and personal interests and offer tuition remission for employees and their dependents. Employees receive complimentary access to the Paul Smith’s College Visitor Interpretive Center (the VIC), which offers 25 miles of scenic hiking, snowshoeing, and cross-country ski trails, wildlife viewing opportunities, educational programs, and interactive butterfly exhibit. For more information or questions about careers at Paul Smith’s College, please call us at: 518-327-6179 Back to Job Openings Seasonal Lean-tos, Cabins & Trails Crew Member Apply Share Link copied Position: Lean-tos, Cabins & Trails Crew Member Status: Part-Time, Non-Exempt, Staff Department: Forestry Reports to: Forest Operations Compensation: $16.00-$17.00 hourly rate Position Description: Paul Smith’s College Lt C & T crew is a fully functioning entity focused on creating, re-designing, and upgrading trails as well as repair and construction of existing lean-tos and cabins on PSC lands as well as contracting to private individuals and/or companies. Our mission is to extend our classroom and field instruction with real world application thereof. This approach will set the standard for exceptional work moving forward regarding how PSC lands shall be developed, and also serve as a springboard for students wishing to continue in this profession and related fields. Accessibility in the form of ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) compliance will also be incorporated on a case-by-case basis. Students will also have the ability to continue in their positions during the academic semesters as the workload allows and they remain in good standing in the academic studies. Advancement opportunities are also available. We also work in tandem with the Forest Ops crew when needed and vise-versa. Duties and Responsibilities: Successful crew members will be instrumental in creating safe, functional, and aesthetically pleasing end products. They will also be responsible for aiding in design and development of projects, including estimating time & material costs needed as well as proper tool use and maintenance. Focus should also include the impacts to the natural environment, ecosystems and visitor experience. Understanding and applying aspects of sustainability and accessibility shall always be in the forefront. Requirements: Parks & Conservation students are encouraged to apply as well as those in the Forestry, Natural Resource Conservation Management, and Sustainability disciplines, however all students are welcome to apply Prior landscaping and/or construction experience, or the willingness and desire to grasp and excel in hands-on woods work Currently possess or have the ability to acquire basic Red Cross First Aid. Experience using numerous landscaping and construction hand tools. (Chainsaw experience is not necessarily required; however, students who operate chainsaws must have Game of Logging levels I & II certifications) Understand and appreciate hazards and safety protocol involved in working in a natural environment Capacity to lift, carry, and maneuver 50lbs, and perform other strenuous activities Ability to work in inclement weather and on uneven terrain Capacity to perform duties independently, proactively, and accept direction from crew boss and/or crew supervisor Valid Driver’s License and a motor vehicle record that meets the College’s standard of “approved driver.” Must provide proof of health insurance. Housing may be available in PSC dorms during the summer months Paul Smith’s College is an equal opportunity employer. Position descriptions are created that focus on essential functions, using inclusive language, and avoid unnecessary or discriminatory requirements. Apply Paul Smith's College Home 7777 NY-30, Paul Smiths, NY 12970 admissions@paulsmiths.edu Phone 888-873-6570 60° F Clear sky Social Facebook Instagram X YouTube TikTok Live Webcams Action Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login Footer About Paul Smith's College Faculty & Staff Directory Graduate Studies Careers Mercantile Library Directions to Paul Smith's College Privacy Policy Request Info News © 2026 Paul Smith's College . All rights reserved Site Menu Search Submit site The Smitty Story Apply for Admission First-Year Applicants Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants International Applicants Graduate Applicants Cost & Aid Tuition & Fees Net Price Calculator Types of Aid Applying for Aid Payment Academics Find a Program Hands-on Learning Work Experience Program Academic Support Meet Our Faculty Graduate Studies M.S. Natural Resources Conservation M.P.S. Sustainable Tourism Management Graduate Certificates Battlefish Academy TopGuide Troops to Timber Life at Paul Smith’s College Housing & Dining Campus Community The Adirondacks Safety & Support Alumni Athletics Search Submit Actions Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login For You Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Close Close"
    },
    "https://www.paulsmiths.edu/careers/?jobId=8e643c74-b49b-0753-4211-0600ceb9e185": {
      "title": "Custodian II",
      "description": "Careers - Paul Smith's College Skip Main Navigation Paul Smith's College Home For You For You Audience Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center & Institutes Centers Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Menu You are here: Home / Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College At Paul Smith’s College, we believe in investing in our employees’ well-being. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that includes not only robust health, dental, and vision insurance, but generous paid time off, including vacation, personal leave, sick leave, and holidays. We prioritize employee wellness through access to a supportive employee assistance program. A myriad of voluntary benefits are available, including short- & long-term disability insurance, accident insurance, critical illness insurance, and legal assistance. We understand the importance of work-life balance and encourage employees to take advantage of our flexible work options when possible. The College offers eligible employees with a 403(b) retirement plan account and contributes 5% of their salary towards the plan. Employees receive complimentary access to our library, gym, athletic facilities, fitness center, recreational equipment, on-campus sporting events, cultural events, and social activities. Discounts are available for on-campus dining and the college’s Mercantile bookstore. We value our employees’ academic and personal interests and offer tuition remission for employees and their dependents. Employees receive complimentary access to the Paul Smith’s College Visitor Interpretive Center (the VIC), which offers 25 miles of scenic hiking, snowshoeing, and cross-country ski trails, wildlife viewing opportunities, educational programs, and interactive butterfly exhibit. For more information or questions about careers at Paul Smith’s College, please call us at: 518-327-6179 Back to Job Openings Custodian II Apply Share Link copied Position: Custodian II Status: Regular, Full-time, Non-Exempt Department: Campus Operations Reports to: Operations Custodial Team Coordinator Salary: $21-23/hour Schedule: Days: 8-hour shifts Tues through Saturday between the hours of 5:30 a.m. – 4:30 p.m. Can flex schedule according to setup needs. Description: The Custodian II position is responsible for maintaining a clean, safe, sanitary, and welcoming campus environment for students, faculty, staff, and visitors. This position will be primarily responsible for cleaning the student center. Coordinating and performing the facilities related responsibilities for event setups in the Student Center as well as those in other areas of the campus will be expected. The role supports the college’s mission by maintaining campus infrastructure and appearance in accordance with Paul Smith’s College operational and customer service standards. Duties & Responsibilities: Managing setups and coordinating with necessary departments to provide facilities support. Provide necessary information and direction for others to perform setups if not able to physically be there. Operate and use heavy equipment in carrying out setups. Clean and sanitize classrooms, offices, residence halls, restrooms, hallways, stairwells, lounges, and public areas. Sweep, mop, vacuum, dust, and polish floors and surfaces. Empty trash and recycling receptacles and transport waste to designated collection points. Clean windows, doors, walls, partitions, and furniture. Restock restroom and custodial supplies. Operate custodial equipment such as vacuums, auto scrubbers, buffers, carpet extractors, and floor machines. Assist with floor care projects including stripping, waxing, buffing, and carpet cleaning. Set up and break down tables, chairs, and equipment for campus events and meetings. Report maintenance concerns, safety hazards, and repair needs to supervisors. Follow all safety procedures regarding OSHA standards. Support emergency response activities including snow events, spills, or campus emergencies as assigned. Maintain positive customer service interactions with students, faculty, staff, and guests. Work collaboratively with team members to complete projects and goals. Perform other duties as assigned by Custodial Evening Lead or Custodial Team Coordinator or designee in their absence. Qualifications: High school diploma or GED required Previous custodial or housekeeping experience preferred but not required Ability to follow verbal and written instructions Ability to work both independently and as part of a team Basic knowledge of cleaning methods, chemicals, and equipment preferred Strong customer service orientation Ability to work with a broad range of diverse individuals and groups to form a safe and inclusive campus community. Active driver’s license and driving record acceptable to our insurance carrier. Physical Requirements: Environmental Conditions Work is consistently performed in indoor academic and residential spaces with normal temperature ranges for the season. Fast-paced environment during academic year and campus events. Traditional noise of a busy workspace setting can be expected. Working spaces may be crowded based on traffic and space configuration. Routine exposure to cleaning chemicals to perform duties. Lifting and Carrying Ability to lift up to 50 lbs. Routine lifting between 10-25 lbs. Frequent lifting and carrying light to moderate weight products or tools (cleaning tools, cleaning products, garbage, furniture items, etc.). Utilize assistive devices and equipment as necessary. Mobility Perform physically active work including bending, walking, climbing stairs, and standing for extended periods. Infrequent needs to reach overhead to perform work duties. Climbing various staircases across the campus to engage in meetings and/or presentations. Utilize mobility assistive devices and equipment as necessary. Dexterity and Hand-Eye Coordination Manual dexterity for handling cleaning tools. Sensory Requirements Good vision is required, with corrective lenses as necessary. Good hearing is required with assistive devices as necessary. Paul Smith’s College is an equal opportunity employer. Position descriptions are created that focus on essential functions, using inclusive language, and avoid unnecessary or discriminatory requirements. Paul Smith's College Home 7777 NY-30, Paul Smiths, NY 12970 admissions@paulsmiths.edu Phone 888-873-6570 42° F Clear sky Social Facebook Instagram X YouTube TikTok Live Webcams Action Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login Footer About Paul Smith's College Faculty & Staff Directory Graduate Studies Careers Mercantile Library Directions to Paul Smith's College Privacy Policy Request Info News © 2026 Paul Smith's College . All rights reserved Site Menu Search Submit site The Smitty Story Apply for Admission First-Year Applicants Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants International Applicants Graduate Applicants Cost & Aid Tuition & Fees Net Price Calculator Types of Aid Applying for Aid Payment Academics Find a Program Hands-on Learning Work Experience Program Academic Support Meet Our Faculty Graduate Studies M.S. Natural Resources Conservation M.P.S. Sustainable Tourism Management Graduate Certificates Battlefish Academy TopGuide Troops to Timber Life at Paul Smith’s College Housing & Dining Campus Community The Adirondacks Safety & Support Alumni Athletics Search Submit Actions Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login For You Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Close Close"
    },
    "https://www.paulsmiths.edu/careers/?jobId=c91bd781-122a-53da-6b3e-d058dc42a567": {
      "title": "Laboratory Analyst",
      "description": "Careers - Paul Smith's College Skip Main Navigation Paul Smith's College Home For You For You Audience Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center & Institutes Centers Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Menu You are here: Home / Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College At Paul Smith’s College, we believe in investing in our employees’ well-being. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that includes not only robust health, dental, and vision insurance, but generous paid time off, including vacation, personal leave, sick leave, and holidays. We prioritize employee wellness through access to a supportive employee assistance program. A myriad of voluntary benefits are available, including short- & long-term disability insurance, accident insurance, critical illness insurance, and legal assistance. We understand the importance of work-life balance and encourage employees to take advantage of our flexible work options when possible. The College offers eligible employees with a 403(b) retirement plan account and contributes 5% of their salary towards the plan. Employees receive complimentary access to our library, gym, athletic facilities, fitness center, recreational equipment, on-campus sporting events, cultural events, and social activities. Discounts are available for on-campus dining and the college’s Mercantile bookstore. We value our employees’ academic and personal interests and offer tuition remission for employees and their dependents. Employees receive complimentary access to the Paul Smith’s College Visitor Interpretive Center (the VIC), which offers 25 miles of scenic hiking, snowshoeing, and cross-country ski trails, wildlife viewing opportunities, educational programs, and interactive butterfly exhibit. For more information or questions about careers at Paul Smith’s College, please call us at: 518-327-6179 Back to Job Openings Laboratory Analyst Apply Share Link copied Position: Laboratory Analyst Status: Regular, Full-time, Exempt Department: Adirondack Watershed Institute (AWI) Reports to: Laboratory Technical Manager Salary: $63,600.00/year Description: The Adirondack Watershed Institute is a program of Paul Smith’s College, a private undergraduate college located in the heart of the Adirondack Park in upstate New York. The mission of AWI is to protect clean water, conserve habitat and support the health and well-being of people in the Adirondacks through scientific inquiry, stewardship, and real-world experiences for students. AWI’s Environmental Research Laboratory holds certification under NYSDOH Environmental Laboratory Accreditation Program (ELAP) The Laboratory Analyst is a mid-level position within AWI. This position serves as an analyst in the environmental research lab and supports general laboratory operations while adhering to the requirements of the ELAP accreditation. Duties & Responsibilities: · Receive, inspect, and log environmental samples into the Laboratory Information Management System (LIMS). Verify sample identification, required documentation, container suitability, preservation, and receipt condition. · Ensure timely preparation, processing, and analysis of environmental samples. Monitor sample holding times and preservation deadlines, prioritize assigned work accordingly, and promptly communicate samples at risk of exceeding laboratory or project requirements. · Prepare and verify analytical standards, reagents, and quality control materials; maintain preparation records, labeling, expiration dates, and traceability to source materials. · Evaluate calibration and batch quality control results against established acceptance criteria; document exceptions and coordinate corrective action or reanalysis with laboratory management. · Compile analytical data packages and assist with reviews. Maintain complete, accurate, and traceable laboratory records, including preparation and analysis dates, dilution factors, calculations, instrument files, and applicable data qualifiers. · Initiate corrective action when needed, notify the Quality Assurance Officer of any issues or preventative actions needed, and participate in root cause analysis. · Troubleshoot analytical problems and identify solutions. · Maintain laboratory instruments and supporting equipment. Perform and document routine equipment performance checks, calibration verifications, preventive maintenance, and temperature monitoring. · Proactively work with lab management to enhance existing laboratory methods, such as improving method performance or reducing run times. Support the development of new laboratory methods. Participate in method detection limit studies, reporting limit verification, instrument capability demonstrations, and method performance evaluations. · Maintain up-to-date staff demonstration of capability for assigned analysis and keep personnel files current, including ongoing training documentation. · Maintain a clean and tidy lab work area, including laboratory dishwashing, and chemical inventory and disposal. · Adhere to safety and quality assurance protocols. · Monitor laboratory supplies and consumables, communicate purchasing needs, and maintain adequate materials for scheduled analyses. · Assist the laboratory management in overseeing and maintaining the Environmental Research Laboratory Quality Assurance Management System to NYS DOH ELAP standards. · Assist the Laboratory Director and Technical Manager with data reporting and delivery to clients. · Assist in training seasonal employees and help to ensure that their work adheres to standard operating procedures. · Participate in annual quality assurance audits led by lab management and lab proficiency testing. · Contribute expertise to new project development. · Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications: · A Bachelor’s degree in environmental science, chemistry, biology, or related field. · Two years of experience conducting lab analysis on environmental water samples. · Familiarity with analytical methods and wet chemistry techniques for nutrients, anions, cations, carbon, pH, conductivity, and alkalinity analysis, for example. · Ability to calibrate and operate lab instruments independently: ICP-OES, FIA, IC, TOC/N, pH/Cond. · Ability to work autonomously and proactively. · Strong computer and data entry skills, especially with spreadsheet, database, and instrumentation software. · High level of attention to detail. · Effective problem-solving abilities. · Ability to work with a broad range of diverse individuals and groups to form a safe and inclusive campus community. Preferred: · One year serving as a lab analyst in an ELAP/NELAC certified laboratory. · Familiarity with the geography of the Adirondack region. · Driver’s license and driving record acceptable to the College’s insurance carrier. Physical Requirements: Environmental Conditions · Work is consistently performed in an indoor laboratory setting with normal temperature ranges for the season. · Traditional noise of a busy office setting can be expected. · Working spaces may be crowded based on traffic and space configuration. Lifting and Carrying · Ability to regularly lift up to 20 lbs. · Frequent lifting and carrying of light to moderate weight items (trays of water sample bottles, glassware, laptop, reagent bottles). Mobility · Ability to bend, stoop, and kneel to pick up items as necessary. · Climbing staircases within buildings and around campus. · Ability to spend extended amounts of time on your feet. Dexterity and Hand-Eye Coordination · Manual dexterity for handling precise laboratory equipment. · Fine motor skills for work performed on laptop. · Clear and legible penmanship. · Ability to reach overhead or below knee level to perform work duties. Sensory Requirements · Good vision, including color vision, with corrective lenses as necessary. · Good hearing with assistive devices as necessary. Paul Smith’s College is an equal opportunity employer. Position descriptions are created that focus on essential functions, using inclusive language, and avoid unnecessary or discriminatory requirements. Paul Smith's College Home 7777 NY-30, Paul Smiths, NY 12970 admissions@paulsmiths.edu Phone 888-873-6570 42° F Clear sky Social Facebook Instagram X YouTube TikTok Live Webcams Action Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login Footer About Paul Smith's College Faculty & Staff Directory Graduate Studies Careers Mercantile Library Directions to Paul Smith's College Privacy Policy Request Info News © 2026 Paul Smith's College . All rights reserved Site Menu Search Submit site The Smitty Story Apply for Admission First-Year Applicants Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants International Applicants Graduate Applicants Cost & Aid Tuition & Fees Net Price Calculator Types of Aid Applying for Aid Payment Academics Find a Program Hands-on Learning Work Experience Program Academic Support Meet Our Faculty Graduate Studies M.S. Natural Resources Conservation M.P.S. Sustainable Tourism Management Graduate Certificates Battlefish Academy TopGuide Troops to Timber Life at Paul Smith’s College Housing & Dining Campus Community The Adirondacks Safety & Support Alumni Athletics Search Submit Actions Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login For You Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Close Close"
    },
    "https://www.paulsmiths.edu/careers/?jobId=9d7f7393-e0fc-5204-0bed-11630bcea882": {
      "title": "AWI Operations and Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "Careers - Paul Smith's College Skip Main Navigation Paul Smith's College Home For You For You Audience Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center & Institutes Centers Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Menu You are here: Home / Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College At Paul Smith’s College, we believe in investing in our employees’ well-being. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that includes not only robust health, dental, and vision insurance, but generous paid time off, including vacation, personal leave, sick leave, and holidays. We prioritize employee wellness through access to a supportive employee assistance program. A myriad of voluntary benefits are available, including short- & long-term disability insurance, accident insurance, critical illness insurance, and legal assistance. We understand the importance of work-life balance and encourage employees to take advantage of our flexible work options when possible. The College offers eligible employees with a 403(b) retirement plan account and contributes 5% of their salary towards the plan. Employees receive complimentary access to our library, gym, athletic facilities, fitness center, recreational equipment, on-campus sporting events, cultural events, and social activities. Discounts are available for on-campus dining and the college’s Mercantile bookstore. We value our employees’ academic and personal interests and offer tuition remission for employees and their dependents. Employees receive complimentary access to the Paul Smith’s College Visitor Interpretive Center (the VIC), which offers 25 miles of scenic hiking, snowshoeing, and cross-country ski trails, wildlife viewing opportunities, educational programs, and interactive butterfly exhibit. For more information or questions about careers at Paul Smith’s College, please call us at: 518-327-6179 Back to Job Openings AWI Operations and Engagement Coordinator Apply Share Link copied Position: Operations and Engagement Coordinator Status: Regular, Full-time, Exempt Department: Adirondack Watershed Institute (AWI) Reports to: Executive Director Salary : $63,600.00/year Description: The Operations and Engagement Coordinator provides administrative, operational, and communications support to the Adirondack Watershed Institute (AWI). This position helps ensure efficient day-to-day operations while supporting organizational communications, outreach, and engagement efforts. The role is responsible for maintaining organizational systems and records, coordinating communications and development efforts, coordinating internal and external events, and assisting with the production of publications, reports, digital content, and marketing materials that advance AWI’s mission. Duties & Responsibilities: Track and maintain organization-wide performance metrics, data, reporting systems, and administrative records. Manage organizational contact databases, mailing lists, donor records, and general correspondence. Establish and maintain grant writing, fundraising, and reporting calendars, assisting with grant proposals and serving as a liaison to the college’s grant office. Coordinate office operations, including purchasing, inventory management, vendor and facility relationships, filing systems, and administrative procedures. Coordinate scheduling and logistics for internal and external meetings, events, workshops, and organizational activities, including calendars, materials, registrations, and follow-up communications. Assist with development and implementation of AWI's communications plans, calendars, and outreach strategies. Create, schedule, and manage content across social media, email marketing, newsletters, websites, blogs, and other communications platforms. Draft, edit, format, and design reports, publications, presentations, newsletters, promotional materials, and other organizational communications. Maintain and update website content, digital resources, and communications materials to ensure accuracy and consistency. Represent AWI as needed at campus, community, and other public-facing events including staffing information tables. Coordinate and organize AWI’s collection of program updates, stories, photos, and impact metrics to support communications, fundraising, grant reporting, annual reporting, and other organizational reporting needs. Provide administrative and operational support to AWI leadership and staff, including project coordination and special initiatives. Perform other duties as assigned by the Executive Director. Qualifications: Associate’s degree or equivalent coursework in communications, marketing, public relations, business administration, environmental studies, or a related field; or equivalent combination of education and experience. One to three years of experience in administrative support, communications, marketing, office management, grant writing, fundraising, and/or related work. Strong computer proficiency, including Microsoft Office Suite, Google Workspace, and Dropbox. Experience managing social media platforms and creating digital communications content. Strong written, verbal, organizational, and interpersonal communication skills. Excellent attention to detail and ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines simultaneously. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound judgment. Ability to work with a broad range of diverse individuals and groups to form a safe, welcoming, and inclusive team and community. Occasional evening or weekend work may be required for events or special projects. Preferred: Bachelor’s degree in communications, marketing, public relations, business administration, environmental science, or a related field. Experience with graphic design platforms. Experience with website content management systems. Experience with email marketing software. Familiarity with nonprofit and environmental organizations. Experience with database management. Experience with fundraising activities, including grant writing and reporting. Physical Requirements: Environmental Conditions Work is consistently performed in an indoor office setting with normal temperature ranges for the season. Traditional noise of a busy office setting can be expected. Working spaces may be crowded based on traffic and space configuration. Lifting and Carrying Ability to regularly lift up to10 lbs. Frequent lifting and carrying of light to moderate office equipment (laptop or meeting materials) weight items. Mobility Prolonged sitting majority of the workday to complete responsibilities. Ability to bend, stoop, and kneel to pick up items as necessary. Ability to travel throughout campus to engage in meetings and/or presentations. Dexterity and Hand-Eye Coordination Manual dexterity for handling small office-related objects. Fine motor skills for work performed on laptop. Sensory Requirements Good vision, including color vision if required, with corrective lenses as necessary. Good hearing with assistive devices as necessary. Paul Smith’s College is an equal opportunity employer. Position descriptions are created that focus on essential functions, using inclusive language, and avoid unnecessary or discriminatory requirements. Paul Smith's College Home 7777 NY-30, Paul Smiths, NY 12970 admissions@paulsmiths.edu Phone 888-873-6570 44° F Clear sky Social Facebook Instagram X YouTube TikTok Live Webcams Action Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login Footer About Paul Smith's College Faculty & Staff Directory Graduate Studies Careers Mercantile Library Directions to Paul Smith's College Privacy Policy Request Info News © 2026 Paul Smith's College . All rights reserved Site Menu Search Submit site The Smitty Story Apply for Admission First-Year Applicants Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants International Applicants Graduate Applicants Cost & Aid Tuition & Fees Net Price Calculator Types of Aid Applying for Aid Payment Academics Find a Program Hands-on Learning Work Experience Program Academic Support Meet Our Faculty Graduate Studies M.S. Natural Resources Conservation M.P.S. Sustainable Tourism Management Graduate Certificates Battlefish Academy TopGuide Troops to Timber Life at Paul Smith’s College Housing & Dining Campus Community The Adirondacks Safety & Support Alumni Athletics Search Submit Actions Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login For You Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Close Close"
    },
    "https://www.paulsmiths.edu/careers/?jobId=0a4be495-4a17-2fc0-07b6-59393bc46832": {
      "title": "Nordic Center Manager",
      "description": "Careers - Paul Smith's College Skip Main Navigation Paul Smith's College Home For You For You Audience Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center & Institutes Centers Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Menu You are here: Home / Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College Jobs at Paul Smith's College At Paul Smith’s College, we believe in investing in our employees’ well-being. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that includes not only robust health, dental, and vision insurance, but generous paid time off, including vacation, personal leave, sick leave, and holidays. We prioritize employee wellness through access to a supportive employee assistance program. A myriad of voluntary benefits are available, including short- & long-term disability insurance, accident insurance, critical illness insurance, and legal assistance. We understand the importance of work-life balance and encourage employees to take advantage of our flexible work options when possible. The College offers eligible employees with a 403(b) retirement plan account and contributes 5% of their salary towards the plan. Employees receive complimentary access to our library, gym, athletic facilities, fitness center, recreational equipment, on-campus sporting events, cultural events, and social activities. Discounts are available for on-campus dining and the college’s Mercantile bookstore. We value our employees’ academic and personal interests and offer tuition remission for employees and their dependents. Employees receive complimentary access to the Paul Smith’s College Visitor Interpretive Center (the VIC), which offers 25 miles of scenic hiking, snowshoeing, and cross-country ski trails, wildlife viewing opportunities, educational programs, and interactive butterfly exhibit. For more information or questions about careers at Paul Smith’s College, please call us at: 518-327-6179 Back to Job Openings Nordic Center Manager Apply Share Link copied Position: Nordic Center Manager Status: Regular, Full-Time, Exempt Department: Paul Smith’s College Outdoor Center / Visitor Interpretive Center (VIC) Reports to: Director, Paul Smith’s College Outdoor Center / VIC Director Salary: $62,353.20-$68,000.00 Position Summary: The VIC/Outdoor Center is a distinctive College asset that advances Paul Smith’s College’s mission through outdoor recreation, environmental and experiential learning, student employment, athletics, community engagement, and prospective-student recruitment. Paul Smith’s College seeks a hands-on Nordic Center Manager to lead and support the continued operation, growth, and integration of the College’s Nordic Center at its Visitor Interpretive Center/Outdoor Center as a welcoming regional destination, event venue, community recreation resource, student-learning environment, and earned-revenue operation. The Manager will help build year-round visibility and use of the VIC/Outdoor Center through Nordic programming, races and events, public and partner engagement, passes and participation growth, and a consistently high-quality visitor experience. Working collaboratively with the VIC Director, Trails & Facilities Manager, and other College partners, the Manager will align daily operations, winter readiness, programming, facility use, and strategic improvements with the College’s broader goals for student experience, enrollment, community relationships, and sustainable earned revenue. Core Responsibilities: Destination, Events, and Programs Support the Nordic Center’s continued development as a welcoming, high-quality regional destination for students, teams, visitors, and community members. Identify, pursue, plan, promote, and support appropriate races, training opportunities, clinics, youth programs, community events, group outings, and other activities that increase use of the Center. Build public programming opportunities, including learn-to-ski opportunities, skills clinics, youth and school programs, camps, guided outings, and community races. Build and maintain productive relationships with Nordic clubs, race directors, coaches, schools, collegiate programs, tourism partners, outdoor-recreation organizations, businesses, and community groups. Contribute to Nordic center visitation and earned-revenue growth through passes, programs, events, facility use, partnerships, sponsorships, and other approved offerings. Operations, Venue, and Workforce Management Coordinate the day-to-day Nordic Center experience, including public access, trail and course readiness, user communications, event and program scheduling, safety procedures, and service standards. Work closely with the VIC Director, Trails & Facilities Manager, Athletics, and other College partners to balance student athlete/team needs, public access, programming, events, equipment, and venue priorities. Identify and recommend trail, course, facility, equipment, signage, and visitor-service improvements that support reliability, accessibility, event readiness, and a positive visitor experience. Recruit, train, schedule, supervise, and mentor student employees, seasonal staff, and volunteers supporting events, programs, visitor services, and Nordic Center operations. Serve as Manager on Duty and assist with other duties as needed or assigned by the Director. Additional Responsibilities: Depending on the experience and strengths of the successful candidate, the position will guide, independently oversee, or collaboratively execute operations within one or more of the following areas: Winter grooming including grooming schedules, trail-condition updates, operating grooming equipment, and maintenance of equipment. Assist and supervise staff and students working on sustainable trail construction and maintenance Advance race recruitment and operations, including regional, high school, collegiate, and club relationships and competition-specific course planning. Expand clinics, camps, youth programming, school partnerships, and inclusive community recreation. Support pass sales, rentals, retail, sponsorships, marketing, tourism partnerships, social media, and other customer-experience or revenue-development initiatives. Long-range planning for trails, courses, facilities, amenities, capital improvements, and the Center’s continued development as a winter destination. Qualifications Required Experience in outdoor recreation, event management, hospitality, community programming, destination development, customer service, Nordic skiing, or a related field. Demonstrated ability to help develop and deliver events, programs, services, partnerships, or customer experiences; strong planning, communication, relationship-building, and problem-solving skills. Ability to supervise and mentor student employees, seasonal staff, volunteers, or crews; ability to work collaboratively across campus and with external partners. Ability to work outdoors in variable winter conditions and to work early mornings, evenings, weekends, holidays, and variable hours as needed. Strong computer and administrative skills, including proficiency with email, spreadsheets, scheduling, and digital recordkeeping; ability to use or quickly learn point-of-sale, rental/reservation, equipment-inventory, trail-condition, and College operational systems. Preferred Experience managing a Nordic center, winter recreation venue, trail system, outdoor-recreation enterprise, event operation, or comparable public-facing operation. Experience with pass or membership programs, registration, earned-revenue services, marketing, sponsorships, tourism partnerships, Nordic events, or cross-country skiing competition. Experience with/strong knowledge of Nordic grooming, snowmobile operation, grooming equipment, trail maintenance, or winter trail operations. Physical Requirements: Must safely navigate icy, snow-covered, and steep trails on foot; must lift and move equipment weighing up to?50 lbs.; must operate or learn to operate outdoor winter utility vehicles, including snowmobiles and tracked UTVs; must possess the physical endurance to respond quickly to situations in deep snow. Paul Smith’s College is an equal opportunity employer. Position descriptions focus on essential functions, use inclusive language, and avoid unnecessary or discriminatory requirements. Submissions accepted through November 20th, 2026 Paul Smith's College Home 7777 NY-30, Paul Smiths, NY 12970 admissions@paulsmiths.edu Phone 888-873-6570 42° F Clear sky Social Facebook Instagram X YouTube TikTok Live Webcams Action Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login Footer About Paul Smith's College Faculty & Staff Directory Graduate Studies Careers Mercantile Library Directions to Paul Smith's College Privacy Policy Request Info News © 2026 Paul Smith's College . All rights reserved Site Menu Search Submit site The Smitty Story Apply for Admission First-Year Applicants Homeschooled Applicants Transfer Applicants International Applicants Graduate Applicants Cost & Aid Tuition & Fees Net Price Calculator Types of Aid Applying for Aid Payment Academics Find a Program Hands-on Learning Work Experience Program Academic Support Meet Our Faculty Graduate Studies M.S. Natural Resources Conservation M.P.S. Sustainable Tourism Management Graduate Certificates Battlefish Academy TopGuide Troops to Timber Life at Paul Smith’s College Housing & Dining Campus Community The Adirondacks Safety & Support Alumni Athletics Search Submit Actions Visit Apply Request Info Give Bobnet Login For You Future Students Current Students Parents & Families Veterans The Adirondacks Alumni Center & Institutes Center for Research, Partnerships, and Innovation Adirondack Watershed Institute Institute of Adventure Hospitality & Food Institute of Forestry John Dillon Park Visitor Interpretive Center Close Close"
